{
  "policy_id": "ld-21-independent-review-for-a-genuine-living",
  "party": "Liberal Democrat",
  "policy_title": "Independent Review for a Genuine Living Wage",
  "outcome": "O14",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O14",
    "direction": "improves",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "This policy commits public sector employers to pay a genuine living wage, which would raise pay for the lowest-paid public sector workers and narrow the gap with higher earners \u2014 but the private sector effect depends on a review's recommendations being adopted, which is not guaranteed.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether the independent review's recommendations would be legislated or remain voluntary for private sector employers, since only public sector adoption is committed.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy establishes an independent review to recommend a genuine living wage and commits government and public sector employers to pay it.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Establish an independent review to recommend a genuine living wage across all sectors, with government departments and all other public sector employers taking a leading role in paying it."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The real Living Wage (\u00a313.45/hr UK, \u00a314.80 London) is notably higher than the legal minimum wage of \u00a312.71/hr.",
        "evidence_id": "E4",
        "quote": "a \"genuine living wage\" would be notably higher than the current legal minimum for many workers"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Approximately 4.4 million UK jobs \u2014 about one in seven \u2014 were paid below the real Living Wage in 2025.",
        "evidence_id": "E5",
        "quote": "approximately 4.4 million jobs in the UK were paid less than the real Living Wage, representing about one in seven jobs (14.6%)"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Since 1999, minimum wage policies have driven pay growth for the lowest earners five times faster than for the highest earners.",
        "evidence_id": "E6",
        "quote": "Since 1999, pay growth for the lowest earners has been five times faster than for the highest earners (1.6% vs 0.3% per year), largely due to minimum wage policies"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "A genuine living wage would likely lead to substantial wage increases for millions of low-paid workers, reducing pay inequality and particularly benefiting women and part-time workers.",
        "evidence_id": "E25",
        "quote": "It would likely lead to substantial wage increases for millions of low-paid workers, helping to reduce in-work poverty and narrow pay inequality, particularly benefiting women and part-time workers"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "NLW increases have had small aggregate employment impacts, but a more substantial increase could test what the labour market can absorb.",
        "evidence_id": "E27",
        "quote": "The experience with the NLW suggests that aggregate employment impacts have been small, but a more substantial increase could test the limits of what the labour market can absorb without more significant job losses"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The policy has two distinct components: (1) a committed instrument \u2014 public sector employers will pay the genuine living wage; and (2) an aspirational instrument \u2014 an independent review to recommend it across all sectors, with no mandate for private sector adoption. For O14, the distributional direction is clear from the evidence: raising the floor wage for the lowest-paid narrows the income gap, as demonstrated by decades of minimum wage policy (E6) and IFS analysis of spillover effects (E7). The committed public sector element will directly raise pay for the subset of public sector workers currently below the genuine living wage, producing a real though limited narrowing of the gap. The cross-sector element is review-only with no committed delivery mechanism, so the soft-verb rule applies: it cannot be scored as delivered. The magnitude is therefore minor rather than moderate \u2014 the public sector commitment is real but covers only a share of the 4.4 million workers below the real Living Wage (E5). Absent this policy, those public sector workers would remain below the genuine living wage floor; the marginal gain is genuine but bounded. Employment risks (E27) could offset some gains if job losses concentrate among lower-income workers, but the prevailing evidence from comparable NLW upratings suggests small aggregate employment effects (E11). Confidence is moderate: the inequality-narrowing mechanism is well-evidenced for mandatory wage floors, but this policy is only partially mandatory.",
